  "production_date": "about 1663-5",
  "brief_description": "Propriety, order and temperance were important virtues in Dutch Calvinist society in the seventeenth century. People admired hard work and disapproved of wantonness. But they also liked a good laugh and the way that Jan Steen could make moralising entertaining.\n\nSome of his jokes are immediately clear. We can see that this is a household where the children and servants are taking over. They are feeding their lunch to the cat, wine to a parrot and roses to the pig, while a small boy reaches into the purse of the sleeping woman. She is the mother of the household, responsible for the well-being of her family. Intoxicated with tobacco and wine, implies Steen, she has neglected her duties. As a result her children run riot, and in the garden beyond a man -- perhaps her husband -- seduces the young woman who is sitting in his lap.",
